# PUTC(3S)

## NAME

putc, putchar, fputc, putw - put character or word on a stream

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int putc(int c, FILE *stream);

int putchar(int c);

int fputc(int c, FILE *stream);

int putw(int w, FILE *stream);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Putc* appends the character *c* to the named output *stream*. It returns the character
written.

*Putchar(c)* is defined as *putc(c, stdout).*

*Fputc* behaves like *putc,* but is a genuine function rather than a macro. It may be used to
save on object text.

*Putw* appends word (i.e. **int**) *w* to the output *stream*. *Putw* neither assumes nor
causes special alignment in the file.

**Note: a word is six bytes here,** not the PDP-11's two, and they are written most significant
first; see getc(3). And *putw* returns 0, or EOF if the stream went bad - v7's page said it
returns the word written, and v7's *putw* in fact returned nothing at all.

The standard stream *stdout* is normally buffered if and only if the output does not refer to a
terminal; this default may be changed by setbuf(3). **Note:** when *stdout does* refer to a
terminal it is *line* buffered here, not unbuffered. v7 had no such mode and spent a system
call per character; see stdio(3) for how the mode works, since the *putc* macro shows no sign
of it.

The standard stream *stderr* is by default unbuffered unconditionally, but use of *freopen*
(see fopen(3)) will cause it to become buffered; *setbuf*, again, will set the state to
whatever is desired. When an output stream is unbuffered information appears on the destination
file or terminal as soon as written; when it is buffered many characters are saved up and
written as a block. *Fflush* (see fclose(3)) may be used to force the block out early.

## SEE ALSO

fopen(3), fclose(3), getc(3), puts(3), printf(3), fread(3), setbuf(3), stdio(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

These functions return the constant **EOF** upon error. Since this is a good integer, ferror(3)
should be used to detect *putw* errors.

## BUGS

Because it is implemented as a macro, *putc* treats a *stream* argument with side effects
improperly. In particular `putc(c, *f++);` doesn't work sensibly. **Note:** C11 sec 7.21.1p3
permits exactly this of *putc* and getc(3), and of no other macro in <stdio.h>.

